---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';import FileDiagram from '../components/FileDiagram.astro';import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';import Arrow from '../components/Arrow.astro';
---
<Layout title="File protection architecture"><PageHero eyebrow="THE TECHNOLOGY" title="Look inside the protection." text="File transformation, cryptographic-material protection and application-level controls work together. Here’s how the architectural pieces relate." number="04"/>
<section class="section container" id="windows"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">WINDOWS IMPLEMENTATION</span><h2>A controlled path<br/>from application to file.</h2></div><p>A file-system filter architecture participates in protection, encryption, decryption and reconstruction.</p></div><div class="windows-architecture" role="img" aria-label="Conceptual Windows file access: requesting application, application-level control, file-system filter with encryption decryption and reconstruction, protected file storage. Application controls may block unauthorized requests."><div class="arch-lane"><span class="arch-layer">APPLICATION</span><div class="arch-box"><span>01</span><strong>Requesting application</strong><small>Open / read / write request</small></div></div><div class="arch-arrow">↓</div><div class="arch-lane"><span class="arch-layer">ACCESS DECISION</span><div class="arch-box green-box"><span>02</span><strong>Application-level controls</strong><small>Evaluate the application against policy</small></div><span class="denial-branch">→ <b>Unapproved request blocked</b></span></div><div class="arch-arrow">↓</div><div class="arch-lane"><span class="arch-layer">FILE-SYSTEM PATH</span><div class="arch-box filter-box"><span>03</span><strong>Windows file-system filter</strong><div class="filter-functions"><span>Protection</span><span>Encryption / decryption</span><span>Reconstruction</span></div></div></div><div class="arch-arrow">↓</div><div class="arch-lane"><span class="arch-layer">STORAGE</span><div class="arch-box"><span>04</span><strong>Protected file data</strong><small>Fragmented and encrypted</small></div></div><p class="small-note">Conceptual relationship of components, not a complete driver specification. Exact control placement and behavior should be confirmed in a technical review.</p></div></section>
<section class="dark-section" id="network"><div class="container section network-detail"><div><span class="outline-tag">UPCOMING NETWORK VERSION</span><h2>Distributed fragments.<br/>HSM-based key architecture.</h2><p class="large-copy">The planned network design brings protected fragments across multiple logical storage locations together with an HSM-based architecture for cryptographic material.</p><p>An HSM has a distinct role from storage. The integration and key lifecycle determine how protected cryptographic material is used in file access.</p><div class="hsm-callout"><svg width="40" height="40" viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="8" y="8" width="24" height="24" stroke="currentColor"/><rect x="14" y="14" width="12" height="12" stroke="currentColor"/><path d="M14 2v6m12-6v6M14 32v6m12-6v6M2 14h6m-6 12h6m24-12h6m-6 12h6" stroke="currentColor"/></svg><div><strong>Hardware security module</strong><span>Key architecture · Separate from file-fragment storage</span></div></div><p class="small-note">HSM model, validation status, integrations and detailed key operations are not specified here. Confirm them for the intended release.</p></div><div><FileDiagram compact/><div class="hsm-boundary"><span>◇ HSM-BASED KEY ARCHITECTURE</span><p>Supports the protection of cryptographic material</p><small>Conceptual relationship · Not a deployment specification</small></div></div></div></section>
<section class="container section"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">SECURITY IS IN THE DETAILS</span><h2>Define the boundaries.<br/>Then test them.</h2></div></div><div class="technical-table-wrap"><table class="technical-table"><caption>Questions for a technical evaluation</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">Described approach</th><th scope="col">What to verify</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Fragment protection</th><td>Fragmentation, encryption and distribution</td><td>Algorithms, fragment dependencies, integrity controls and storage isolation</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Key architecture</th><td>Protected cryptographic material; HSM-based network design upcoming</td><td>Key generation, permitted operations, rotation, revocation and recovery</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Application access</th><td>Application-level controls in the protected access path</td><td>Application identity, allow-list management, enforcement and bypass resistance</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Windows integration</th><td>File-system filter architecture</td><td>Supported Windows versions, application compatibility, performance and failure modes</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Operational resilience</th><td>File-level protection within a broader security program</td><td>Backup/restore behavior, storage outages, key availability and incident procedures</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
<section class="soft-section"><div class="section container split-content"><div><span class="eyebrow">ZERO TRUST, PRECISELY</span><h2>Make the access<br/>decision explicit.</h2></div><div><p class="large-copy">Application-level checks can support a strategy that avoids implicit trust in every process on a system.</p><p>Zero trust is a broader architectural approach. File protection alone does not establish a complete zero-trust architecture or replace identity, device, network and operational controls.</p><a class="text-link" href="/request-demo?interest=Technical%20architecture">Discuss your trust boundaries <Arrow/></a></div></div></section><CTA title="Go deeper than the diagram." text="Bring your security architect. Walk through the implementation questions that matter to your environment."/></Layout>
